Abuela dressed for the work.
   I’d seen her in a housedress, a robe, and a dish towel. Wednesday at
six she came out of her room in a plain dark dress with her hair pulled
back hard, sandals on, a straw bag over her arm, and looked at me the
way a surgeon looks at a very small, very unprepared nurse.
   “Vístete,” she said. “Hoy no se cuenta. Hoy se oye.”
   Today it isn’t told. Today it’s heard.
   Wilfredo’s cab was already at the curb. “She called me at five,” he
said, with his medal swinging. “Nobody calls me at five unless it’s a
baby or a funeral.”
   “Which is this?”
   “Both, probably.”
   Abuela took the front seat and conducted traffic for forty minutes.
She didn’t speak. She raised one finger when he was going too fast and
lowered it when he wasn’t, and by the end of the highway Wilfredo
had stopped looking in the mirror and started looking at her hand.



